            <ArticleTitle>Characterization of &quot;peak E,&quot; a novel amino acid associated with eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1707-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Epidemiologic studies strongly associate eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome (EMS) with ingestion of tryptophan containing a contaminant (&quot;peak E&quot;). Prior reports have suggested that peak E is the di-tryptophan N alpha-animal of acetaldehyde. Spectral and chemical studies now demonstrate that peak E is 1,1'-ethylidenebis[tryptophan]. This novel amino acid may be the etiological agent responsible for EMS, or it may be a marker of a still unidentified causal agent.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
